WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.296
Hi, I'm Marline!

00:00:02.296 --> 00:00:06.321
Today: how NVIDIA's small Dory model thinks harder by running the same

00:00:06.321 --> 00:00:07.662
layers more than once.

00:00:08.933 --> 00:00:13.332
Dory keeps one shared block of twenty layers and runs it several

00:00:13.332 --> 00:00:14.798
times for every token.

00:00:14.798 --> 00:00:16.998
Extra depth costs compute, not parameters.

00:00:18.667 --> 00:00:22.554
A token first goes through eight input layers, each one attention plus

00:00:22.554 --> 00:00:23.850
a feed forward network.

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:26.522
Then the twenty layer recurrent block runs.

00:00:26.522 --> 00:00:30.061
By default it runs twice, with the same weights both times.

00:00:30.481 --> 00:00:34.079
Before every pass, the input block's output is added back, so the

00:00:34.079 --> 00:00:35.579
loop keeps its starting point.

00:00:35.999 --> 00:00:39.208
Eight output layers turn the final state into the next token.

00:00:40.925 --> 00:00:42.506
Here is NVIDIA's own drawing.

00:00:42.506 --> 00:00:45.035
The input block, I, makes the starting state.

00:00:45.455 --> 00:00:48.458
Each loop adds that input back in and runs the shared block

00:00:48.458 --> 00:00:50.459
R again, from loop one to loop k.

00:00:52.192 --> 00:00:55.593
By default, every pass keeps its own keys and values, so the

00:00:55.593 --> 00:00:57.010
cache grows with each loop.

00:00:57.430 --> 00:01:00.350
In last loop mode, each pass overwrites the one before.

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:03.853
The cache stays at the one loop size, whatever the loop count.

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:06.710
The parameters never change.

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:10.940
The work per token does: each extra pass adds about one point

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:12.350
eight billion multiply adds.

00:01:13.675 --> 00:01:16.630
Most of the reported gain comes from the second pass.

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:20.176
A fourth adds a little on agent tasks, and nothing on recall.

00:01:21.517 --> 00:01:24.556
Looping buys more thinking per token, not more facts.

00:01:24.556 --> 00:01:27.596
Reasoning scores rise, and the knowledge score stays flat.

00:01:29.983 --> 00:01:32.677
One shared block, run up to four times.

00:01:32.677 --> 00:01:36.718
Fixed parameters, growing compute, and a cache mode that keeps memory flat.

00:01:37.137 --> 00:01:39.150
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:39.725
I'm Marline.

00:01:39.725 --> 00:01:40.013
Bye!
